<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitutional Convention (United States)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1787">1787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Philadelphia Convention</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitutional Convention met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> from May to September 1787 to revise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>, but instead drafted a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a>. Presided over by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-madison">James Madison</a> as its leading architect, its delegates agreed compromises over representation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and sent the document to the states for ratification.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitutional-convention-united-states.jpg" alt="Constitutional Convention (United States)" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Constitutional Convention · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:United_States_Constitution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Paris of 1783 recognised independence, with a western border on the Mississippi, debts and unrest such as Shays' Rebellion of 1786–1787 persuaded leaders to call the <b>Constitutional Convention</b> at Philadelphia in 1787.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In a presidential democracy, devised at the <b>Constitutional Convention</b> of 1787, a separately elected president serves a fixed term and each branch checks the others.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>Pennsylvania, where the <b>Philadelphia Convention</b> had drafted the Constitution earlier in the year, became the second state to ratify it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-12">12 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Constitution, drafted by the <b>Philadelphia Convention</b> in 1787, had replaced the Articles of Confederation in March 1789.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
